steps.quote:
Human recordings (orange) provide head images and EEF actions; robot data (blue) add wrist images and joint actions. The backbone predicts discrete FAST tokens of the EEF chunk (
$\mathcal{L}_{\text{FAST}}$
) as an auxiliary task alongside the sub-task (
$\mathcal{L}_{\text{text}}$
) and future frames (
$\mathcal{L}_{\text{video}}$
). A shared flow-matching expert reads the backbone at every layer and independently denoises an EEF and a joint chunk, isolated by the attention mask. At deployment, training-only heads (dashed) are omitted, and either stream can drive the robot: the joint stream with robot demos, and the EEF stream for human-to-robot transfer.
